<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nripendra Narayan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nripendra Narayan was maharaja of Cooch Behar, a princely state in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, from 1863 to 1911. Educated under British guardianship, he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suniti-devi">Suniti Devi</a>, daughter of the Brahmo reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keshab-chandra-sen">Keshab Chandra Sen</a>, in 1878. He modernised the state's administration and schools and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cooch-behar-palace">Cooch Behar Palace</a>, modelled on Buckingham Palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nripendra-narayan.jpg" alt="Nripendra Narayan" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lafayette Studio (original); colorized by Regalhist5 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaja_Nripendra_Narayan_(%E0%A6%A8%E0%A7%83%E0%A6%AA%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%A8%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%A6%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%B0_%E0%A6%A8%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BC%E0%A6%A8)_%E2%80%93_Colorized,_c._1902.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
